`len(s)` and the empty string
=============================

`len(s)` on a `string` returns the byte length of the
underlying buffer. For an ASCII string this matches the
character count; for UTF-8 multi-byte content it does not
(see `string_concat_basic.lsysl` for the UTF-8 byte case).

This file pins the small, frequently-relied-on length-and-
emptiness surface: `len("")` is zero; an empty literal is
equal to another empty literal; `len(s)` is stable across
reads and across local re-binding; `len` of a function-
returned string is the byte length of what was returned;
`len` of a concat result equals `len(a) + len(b)` for ASCII.

The static-empty-literal-refcount detail (the runtime stores
literal `""` with sentinel refcount -1 so it's never freed)
is internal — observable only as "no crash when you let
many references to `""` go out of scope," which every
backend handles silently.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

`len("")` is 0
--------------

The most basic case: the empty literal has byte length 0.

    #test
    test_len_empty_literal() -> unit
        val s = ""
        assert(len(s) == 0, s"len(\"\") should be 0, got ${len(s)}")

`len` on single-char ASCII
--------------------------

A one-byte ASCII literal has byte length 1. Pins that `len`
returns the byte count, not 0 (off-by-one) or 2 (NUL-
inclusive).

    #test
    test_len_single_char() -> unit
        val s = "x"
        assert(len(s) == 1, s"len('x') should be 1, got ${len(s)}")

`len` on a five-char literal
----------------------------

`"hello"` is five ASCII bytes. Pins the codegen length is
populated by the string-literal lowering, not e.g. the
`s + ""` round-trip.

    #test
    test_len_five_chars() -> unit
        val s = "hello"
        assert(len(s) == 5, s"len(\"hello\") should be 5, got ${len(s)}")

Two empty literals compare equal
--------------------------------

`"" == ""` is true. Two independently-constructed empty
literals must compare byte-equal. (If a backend miscoded the
empty case as "any zero-length buffer," this still passes;
that's the intended semantics.)

    #test
    test_two_empty_literals_equal() -> unit
        val a = ""
        val b = ""
        assert(a == b, "two empty literals should compare equal")
        assert(len(a) == 0 && len(b) == 0, "both have len 0")

`len(s)` is stable across reads
-------------------------------

Calling `len(s)` twice on the same string returns the same
value. Pins that `len` is a pure read — no buffer mutation,
no side-effect that would change the length.

    #test
    test_len_stable_across_reads() -> unit
        val s = "stable"
        val a = len(s)
        val b = len(s)
        assert(a == b, s"len(s) should be stable: first=$a, second=$b")
        assert(a == 6, s"len(\"stable\") should be 6, got $a")

`len` on a function-returned string
-----------------------------------

`len` of a string produced by a function call is the byte
length of the returned value. Pins that the return-value
ABI preserves the length field of the string descriptor.

    sle_make_label() -> string = "label"

    #test
    test_len_of_returned_string() -> unit
        val s = sle_make_label()
        assert(len(s) == 5, s"len of returned string should be 5, got ${len(s)}")
        assert(s == "label", s"returned string should be 'label', got '$s'")

`len` of an empty function return
---------------------------------

A function that returns `""` produces a length-0 string;
`len` of that result is 0. Pins the empty-return case
separately because some ABIs special-case zero-length
strings differently.

    sle_empty() -> string = ""

    #test
    test_len_of_returned_empty() -> unit
        val s = sle_empty()
        assert(len(s) == 0, s"len of \"\" return should be 0, got ${len(s)}")
        assert(s == "", "empty return should equal \"\"")

`len` after rebinding to another string
---------------------------------------

A `var s` rebound to a different literal must report the new
length, not the old one. Pins that `len(s)` reads from the
current value, not a stashed copy of the original descriptor.

    #test
    test_len_after_rebind() -> unit
        var s = "short"
        assert(len(s) == 5, "initial length")
        s = "much longer string"
        assert(len(s) == 18, s"after rebind, len should be 18, got ${len(s)}")
        s = ""
        assert(len(s) == 0, "after rebind to empty, len should be 0")

`len(a + b)` equals the sum for ASCII
-------------------------------------

For ASCII operands, the result of `+` has byte length equal
to the sum of operand lengths. (Cross-listed in
`string_concat_basic.lsysl`; included here as the bridging
case between empty-length and the concat surface.)

    #test
    test_len_of_concat_sums() -> unit
        val a = ""
        val b = "data"
        val c = a + b
        assert(len(c) == len(a) + len(b), "len(a+b) = len(a)+len(b)")
        assert(len(c) == 4, s"empty + 'data' should be len 4, got ${len(c)}")
        assert(c == "data", s"got '$c'")
